1.⁠ ⁠Retail: The Experience vs. Value Map
In retail, you are usually fighting over the customer's wallet share based on how much they pay versus how much "effort" or "prestige" is involved.

X-Axis: Price Point (Discount/Mass Market → Luxury/Premium)

Y-Axis: Service Level/Experience (Self-Service/Online → High-Touch/Boutique)

The Goal: Find the "White Space." For example, if everyone is "High Price/High Service," there might be an opening for "High Price/Automated Experience" (like high-end vending or tech-first luxury).

2.⁠ ⁠Fintech: The Trust vs. Innovation Map
Fintech is a battle between "The Old Guard" (secure but slow) and "The Disruptors" (fast but unproven).

X-Axis: Product Scope (Niche/Single-Feature → Full Ecosystem/Super-App)

Y-Axis: Regulatory/Trust Profile (Unlicensed/Startup → Fully Licensed/Incumbent Bank)

Alternative: Use User Accessibility (X) vs. Feature Complexity (Y). This helps distinguish between a simple "Round-up savings app" and a "Complex crypto-derivative platform."

3.⁠ ⁠Logistics: The Scale vs. Specialized Tech Map
Logistics is a game of "Physical Assets" (trucks/warehouses) versus "Digital Intelligence" (routing AI/visibility).

X-Axis: Geographic Reach (Local/Last-Mile → Global/Long-Haul)

Y-Axis: Asset Intensity (Asset-Light/Software-Only → Asset-Heavy/Fleet Owners)

Why this works: It separates the 4PLs (Fourth-Party Logistics providers who just manage data) from the 3PLs (who own the actual trucks and ships).
